 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the remaining keys
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Supersedes: §stable-tab-keys
+- Decision: keys are always contiguous (`t1,t2,t3`, in tab order). `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs after a close
+  and resets `next` to the tab count + 1, so the next opened tab gets the next contiguous key. Maintainer decision,
+  made explicitly over §stable-tab-keys.
+- Forbidden: leaving gaps in the keys after a close; leaving `next` out of step with the renumbered tabs.
+- Reason: the key is the URL state (`?tabs=t1,t2,t3`) and the maintainer requires it to stay contiguous.
+- Cost accepted: the key is also the React key, so every tab to the right of a closed one gets a new key and remounts
+  (the scroll position and unsaved form input loss recorded in §stable-tab-keys). Any per-tab state held outside
+  this module and keyed by tab key (e.g. an active-tab key) must be remapped after `closeTab`; the state here has none.
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+
+### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others [SUPERSEDED 2026-10-23 by §contiguous-tab-keys]
 - Since: 2026-09-02
+- Superseded: 2026-10-23 — the maintainer overrode this decision; `closeTab` now renumbers. Kept for its reasoning.
 - Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
 - Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
 - Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
   and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` (the "close keeps keys" test was replaced by the renumbering tests)
